{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 45, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a prominent figure in the 12th century known for his military leadership and political acumen. He ruled as Count of Tripoli from 1155 until his death in 1193. His reign marked a period of significant expansion and consolidation of Tripoli's territory and power. He inherited the countship at a young age facing challenges from both internal factions and external threats particularly from the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the Seljuk Sultanate of Rum.\n\n*Early Life and Ascendancy* Raymond III was the son of Raymond IV Count of Tripoli and his wife Gertrude of Jerusalem. He received a comprehensive education including military training and diplomatic skills. His early years were marked by political maneuvering and struggles for power within the countship. He skillfully navigated these complexities ultimately securing his position as Count.  He proved to be a capable administrator and a shrewd negotiator balancing the interests of the various factions within Tripoli.  He also fostered strong relationships with other powerful rulers in the Levant solidifying Tripoli’s position as a regional power.\n\nRaymond III’s reign was characterized by military campaigns and territorial expansion. He successfully defended Tripoli against numerous sieges and attacks.  He expanded the countship’s territory significantly acquiring new lands and resources.  His military successes earned him considerable prestige and loyalty among his vassals and subjects.  He focused on strengthening Tripoli’s defenses building fortifications and establishing a well-trained army.  He also actively participated in the Crusades though his involvement was often strategic rather than directly combative.\n\n*Political and Economic Policies* Raymond III implemented various policies to strengthen Tripoli’s economy and improve the lives of its citizens. He promoted trade and commerce fostering economic growth within the countship. He invested in infrastructure building roads and bridges to facilitate transportation and communication. He also implemented legal reforms aimed at ensuring justice and maintaining order within Tripoli. He supported local artisans and merchants encouraging the development of crafts and industries. He maintained a strong relationship with the local population providing protection and stability.  His economic policies contributed to the prosperity of Tripoli and enhanced its standing in the region.\n\nRaymond III died in 1193 leaving behind a stable and prosperous countship. His legacy as a strong military leader and wise ruler ensured Tripoli’s continued importance in the Levant for decades to come. He is remembered as a key figure in the history of the Crusader states and a significant contributor to the development of the region. He fostered a period of relative peace and prosperity that allowed Tripoli to flourish as a center of trade and culture. His reign represents a golden age for the countship of Tripoli.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.1804991215467453}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": -1.6689286894688848e-06}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -6.115249561844394e-05}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -2.861018856492592e-06}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁was", "logprob": -0.08474750816822052}, {"token": "▁a", "logprob": -0.03216259181499481}, {"token": "▁prominent", "logprob": -1.361245036125183}, {"token": "▁figure", "logprob": -0.020858105272054672}, {"token": "▁in", "logprob": -0.00552590936422348}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.027455471456050873}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -0.8768133521080017}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.0016796779818832874}, {"token": "th", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁century", "logprob": -0.13149943947792053}, {"token": "▁known", "logprob": -1.4105600118637085}, {"token": "▁for", "logp